Chapter: HostedEcho

 A hosted server: bind, listen, accept one connection, echo what arrives on it,
 close both descriptors. It has no `.expected` sidecar deliberately, because it
 cannot be graded by running it and reading its output: it answers a CLIENT, so
 its harness is codex/plugs/elf/hosted-socket-arm.ps1, which speaks to it.

 The selector values below are the ones the emitter names in
 codex/compiler/Emit/X86_64Boot.codex. They are spelled again here because a
 program reaches the builtin, not the compiler's own constants.

Section: Selectors

  op-socket : Integer = 0
  op-bind : Integer = 1
  op-listen : Integer = 2
  op-accept : Integer = 3
  op-close : Integer = 4
  op-recv : Integer = 5
  op-send : Integer = 6
  op-reuseaddr : Integer = 7

 THE TWO TARGETS MUST NOT SHARE A PORT. WSL2 forwards a listener inside WSL
 onto the Windows host and does not release it when the subject exits, so a
 Windows run following a Linux run on one port answers bind -1 while the same
 binary standalone answers bind 0. `hosted-kind` is 1 on Linux and 2 on
 Windows, so adding it gives each target its own port by construction rather
 than by the harness waiting and hoping.

  echo-port-base : Integer = 34567
  echo-buffer-bytes : Integer = 1024

 THE DESCRIPTOR'S VALUE IS NOT PRINTED, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF PRINTING
 "socket ok" INSTEAD. Every other line here is an answer the two targets must
 agree on, but a descriptor cannot be: Linux hands out a small file descriptor
 (3) and Windows a kernel SOCKET handle (224, and a different one per run).
 Printing it made the two targets' output differ on a value that is not
 comparable, which would either fail the parity arm forever or force the arm to
 exclude a line, and an arm with an exclusion in it is one nobody trusts. What
 is actually being asserted is that the call answered a usable descriptor.

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console, Network.Write] Nothing = act
    let buf = alloc-bytes echo-buffer-bytes
    in let echo-port = echo-port-base + hosted-kind
    in act
      fd <- host-socket op-socket 0 0 0
      ru <- host-socket op-reuseaddr fd 0 0
      bd <- host-socket op-bind fd echo-port 0
      ls <- host-socket op-listen fd 16 0
      print-line-uni (if fd >= 0 then "socket ok" else "socket failed")
      print-line-uni ("reuseaddr " & integer-to-text ru)
      print-line-uni ("bind " & integer-to-text bd)
      print-line-uni ("listen " & integer-to-text ls)
      cfd <- host-socket op-accept fd 0 0
      n <- host-socket op-recv cfd buf echo-buffer-bytes
      st <- host-socket op-send cfd buf n
      c1 <- host-socket op-close cfd
      c2 <- host-socket op-close fd
      print-line-uni ("echoed " & integer-to-text st)
    end
